### Bug type

Behavior bug (incorrect output/state without crash)

### Summary

Adding any named account under `channels.slack.accounts` causes the implicit "default" account (previously served by the top-level `channels.slack.botToken`/`appToken`) to silently stop resolving. The gateway logs an ERROR-level secret-resolution failure and the default Slack channel fails to start, even though the top-level fields are still present and previously worked.

This is the same underlying bug class as the already-fixed Feishu issue (#96929 / fixed by #96935) — the fix apparently didn't cover the Slack (or a shared generic) channel-secrets path.

### Steps to reproduce

1. Start with a working single-account Slack config:
   ```json
   "channels": {
     "slack": {
       "enabled": true,
       "botToken": { "source": "env", "provider": "default", "id": "SLACK_BOT_TOKEN" },
       "appToken": { "source": "env", "provider": "default", "id": "SLACK_APP_TOKEN" }
     }
   }
   ```
2. Add a second named account (documented, supported multi-account pattern — "one channel account per agent" per https://docs.openclaw.ai/concepts/agent-bindings):
   ```json
   "channels": {
     "slack": {
       "enabled": true,
       "botToken": { "source": "env", "provider": "default", "id": "SLACK_BOT_TOKEN" },
       "appToken": { "source": "env", "provider": "default", "id": "SLACK_APP_TOKEN" },
       "accounts": {
         "sage": {
           "botToken": { "source": "env", "provider": "default", "id": "SAGE_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN" },
           "appToken": { "source": "env", "provider": "default", "id": "SAGE_SLACK_APP_TOKEN" }
         }
       }
     }
   }
   ```
3. Restart the gateway.

### Expected behavior

The top-level `botToken`/`appToken` continue to be treated as the implicit `default` account (as they were before any named accounts existed), alongside the newly added `sage` account. Both accounts start successfully.

### Actual behavior

The default account fails to start:

```
[secrets] [SECRETS_REF_IGNORED_INACTIVE_SURFACE] channels.slack.botToken: no enabled account inherits this top-level Slack botToken.
[secrets] [SECRETS_REF_IGNORED_INACTIVE_SURFACE] channels.slack.appToken: no enabled Slack socket-mode surface inherits this top-level appToken.
[slack] channel startup failed: channels.slack.accounts.default.botToken: unresolved SecretRef "env:default:SLACK_BOT_TOKEN". Resolve this command against an active gateway runtime snapshot before reading it.
```

Every other agent/user who was relying on the default Slack account loses Slack access entirely as soon as the first named account is added — this is a real production outage risk for anyone following the documented multi-account pattern.

### Workaround

Explicitly duplicate the top-level tokens under `channels.slack.accounts.default`:
```json
"accounts": {
  "sage": { "botToken": {...}, "appToken": {...} },
  "default": {
    "botToken": { "source": "env", "provider": "default", "id": "SLACK_BOT_TOKEN" },
    "appToken": { "source": "env", "provider": "default", "id": "SLACK_APP_TOKEN" }
  }
}
```
This resolves the startup error, but shouldn't be necessary — it's redundant configuration working around what looks like a real gap in default-account synthesis.

### Suspected root cause (from source inspection, not confirmed by a maintainer)

`resolveChannelAccountSurface` (`src/secrets/channel-secret-basic-runtime.ts:58-86` in the `openclaw/openclaw` monorepo) only synthesizes an implicit `default` account when `accounts` is **empty**:

```ts
if (!isRecord(accounts) || Object.keys(accounts).length === 0) {
  return { hasExplicitAccounts: false, ..., accounts: [{ accountId: "default", account: channel, ... }] };
}
```

But the Slack plugin's `listSlackAccountIds` still yields `["default", "sage"]` once `sage` is added (matching the multi-account docs). The secrets layer sees only `sage` under `accounts`, concludes nothing inherits the top-level `botToken`, marks it inactive, and never resolves it for the implicit `default` — which then dies on `unresolved SecretRef`.

### OpenClaw version

2026.7.1

### Slack plugin version

@openclaw/slack@2026.6.9

### Operating system

Ubuntu (EC2, self-hosted Docker deployment)

### Additional context

Ran into this live while wiring up a per-agent dedicated Slack app on top of an existing shared account, following the documented agent-bindings pattern. Happy to provide the full `openclaw.json` channels/bindings block and reproduction logs if useful.

